ML_PAGE("l6-subjective", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l6\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 6 · SUBJECTIVE EXAM PREPARATION</span><h2>Writing Answers That Earn Marks</h2><p>Define, explain the mechanism, apply it to the scenario, then evaluate and conclude. Each mock question below has its full model answer behind a reveal; the complete subjective notes are underneath.</p></header><div class=\"theme-l6\"><div class=\"card accent-card mb-20\">\n    <h3>The four-move answer (10-20 mark questions)</h3>\n    <div class=\"flow-grid\">\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>1. Establish the principle</b><p>Define the concept accurately in the chapter's terms: residual claim, total return, dilution, split, par, book, market and investment value, EPS, dividend yield, payout ratio, currency return.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>2. Explain the mechanism</b><p>Show <em>why</em>: who is paid first, which way the cash moves, which denominator changed, how the two foreign returns compound.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>3. Apply and analyse</b><p>Use the scenario's numbers with the formula written out ($HPR$, $P \\times N$, $\\dfrac{D}{P}$, $\\dfrac{D}{EPS}$, ownership percentage), then say what each result measures and what it does not.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>4. Evaluate and conclude</b><p>Test the claim against the investor's horizon, liquidity, loss capacity and diversification; name the missing evidence and give a justified, conditional conclusion.</p></div>\n    </div>\n    <p class=\"card-note\">Strong Chapter 6 answers follow one chain: what the investor owns → how value or income arises → what could go wrong → whether it fits this particular investor.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"compare-box span-all\">\n    <div class=\"compare-side\">\n        <h4>Weak answer</h4>\n        <p><b>Q:</b> Evaluate: \"A company paying the highest dividend yield is necessarily the safest income investment.\"</p>\n        <p>\"I agree, because a high dividend yield means the investor receives more income every year, so it is the safest choice for an income investor.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-weak\">Treats yield as guaranteed income; never asks why the yield is high, whether it is sustainable, or what the investor needs.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"compare-side highlight\">\n        <h4>Strong answer</h4>\n        <p>\"The claim should be rejected because of the word 'necessarily'. Dividend yield is the annual dividend divided by the current price, so it can rise simply because the price has fallen: a $\\text{RM}0.80$ dividend on a share that drops from $\\text{RM}20$ to $\\text{RM}10$ moves the yield from $4\\%$ to $8\\%$, possibly because the company's prospects have deteriorated. A dividend is not a contractual payment; a high payout relative to earnings or weak cash generation may make it hard to sustain, although these measures alone cannot prove a cut. The investor should examine earnings, cash flow, debt and dividend history, consider diversification, and compare the uncertainty of both the income and the principal with how much stable income they actually need. Equity income is never risk-free.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-good\">Principle (definition of yield) + mechanism (falling denominator) + numbers + sustainability evidence + investor-specific, conditional conclusion.</p>\n    </div>\n</div>\n</div><h3 class=\"block-title\">Mock examination: questions with model answers</h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><span>Equity ownership and investment performance (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A company issues ordinary shares to the public. A buyer purchases 200 shares at RM20 each. One year later the shares are worth RM22 each, and the investor has received RM0.60 cash dividend per share. Ignore tax and trading costs.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [4] Explain why ordinary shareholders are called residual owners and compare their claim with that of lenders.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [6] Calculate the total dividend, capital gain, total ringgit return, and holding-period return.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Explain two reasons why the investor's positive one-year result does not guarantee future results. Distinguish company-specific from market-wide risk.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Evaluate the claim: \"Stocks are always the best place for money needed next year because they tend to do well over long periods.\"</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Understand → Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Residual claim [4].</strong> Ordinary shareholders own the equity remaining after the firm's obligations and senior claims. Lenders have contractual claims to interest and repayment; shareholders do not have a comparable guarantee of dividend or principal. If the company is liquidated, shareholders receive value only after prior claims are met. Shareholders may benefit from growth in the residual value, but could lose their investment. <em>Illustrative marking: 2 marks for the claim and 2 for an accurate lender comparison.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Returns [6].</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Initial investment = $200\\times RM20=RM4,000$.</li>\n<li>Dividend = $200\\times RM0.60=RM120$.</li>\n<li>Capital gain = $200\\times(RM22-RM20)=RM400$.</li>\n<li>Total return = $RM120+RM400=RM520$.</li>\n<li>Holding-period return = $RM520/RM4,000=13\\%$.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The 13% includes 10% price appreciation plus 3% dividend income, based on the initial price. <em>Illustrative marking: dividend 1, capital gain 1, total 1, formula and result 2, interpretation 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Uncertainty [5].</strong> The company might lose a major customer or its management might make a poor decision: these are company-specific risks. An economy-wide recession or market repricing can affect many companies simultaneously: this is market-wide risk. Diversifying may reduce company-specific exposure but cannot remove market-wide risk. Last year's price change and dividend are historical observations, not promises about next year. <em>Illustrative marking: 2 for each risk with mechanism, 1 for the historical-return limitation.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Decision [5].</strong> Reject the absolute claim. Long-run historical performance does not prevent a substantial decline over a particular year. If the money must be available next year, liquidity and the ability to tolerate loss matter more than simply maximizing expected stock return. A diversified stock allocation might still have a role for money with a longer horizon, depending on circumstances; a near-term spending reserve requires a separate assessment of lower-risk alternatives and purchasing-power risk. <em>Illustrative marking: conclusion 1, horizon/liquidity 2, risk and qualified alternative 2.</em></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><span>Share issuance, rights and corporate actions (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Meridian has 1,000,000 ordinary shares outstanding. Mira owns 10,000. The company offers existing holders one new share at RM4 for every four shares held. Assume every eligible holder takes up their full entitlement and no other shares are issued.</p>\n<p>Later, after the rights issue, a shareholder holds 2,000 shares trading at RM12 each immediately before a 2-for-1 split. Ignore market movements and costs for the split calculation.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [5] Calculate Mira's percentage ownership before and after fully subscribing. Explain the purpose and a limitation of rights offerings.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [3] Calculate Mira's percentage ownership if all other holders subscribe fully but she buys no new shares.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Calculate the shareholder's post-split number of shares, theoretical price per share and total holding value. Does the split create wealth by itself?</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [4] Explain a spinoff and compare a share repurchase with issuing new shares in terms of outstanding shares and cash flows.</p>\n<p><strong>(e)</strong> [3] Explain why ownership percentage alone may not reveal voting control if common stock has multiple classes.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Rights [5].</strong> Initially Mira owns $10,000/1,000,000=1\\%$. Her entitlement is $10,000/4=2,500$ shares, costing $2,500\\times RM4=RM10,000$. All holders together subscribe to $1,000,000/4=250,000$ shares. Afterward she owns $12,500$ out of $1,250,000$, still <strong>1%</strong>. A rights offering lets existing owners participate proportionally in new financing and can limit ownership dilution if they subscribe; participation requires cash, and the market value of the right or eventual value of the new shares is not guaranteed. <em>Illustrative marking: entitlement 1, totals 1, both ownership percentages 1, purpose 1, limitation 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) No subscription [3].</strong> Other holders own 990,000 shares and subscribe to $990,000/4=247,500$ new shares. Total outstanding become $1,247,500$; Mira retains 10,000 and owns $10,000/1,247,500\\approx\\mathbf{0.8016\\%}$. This decrease is ownership dilution. The calculation assumes Mira's unsubscribed entitlement is not reallocated; a real offering's terms may differ. <em>Illustrative marking: new share count 1, denominator 1, percentage and interpretation 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Split [5].</strong> The 2,000 shares become 4,000. The theoretical price halves to RM6, so the value remains $4,000\\times RM6=RM24,000$, the same as $2,000\\times RM12$. A split changes units, not the company's assets or the investor's proportional ownership by itself. Actual trading prices can subsequently change. <em>Illustrative marking: shares 1, price 1, value 1, mechanism 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Spinoff and buyback [4].</strong> A spinoff separates a subsidiary into an independent company, distributing its shares to existing owners under the transaction terms. A company issuing new shares generally receives capital and increases outstanding shares; repurchasing shares generally pays out company cash and reduces shares outstanding while held in treasury or retired, depending on rules and treatment. Effects on per-share value depend on price and financing, not solely on share count. <em>Illustrative marking: spinoff 2, issuance versus buyback 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(e) Voting [3].</strong> Classes can have different votes per share or other rights. A small economic ownership percentage can therefore command a disproportionately large voting percentage. Read the class terms before inferring control. <em>Illustrative marking: differentiated rights 2, implication 1.</em></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><span>Stock values, quotes and costs (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Orion has total assets of RM180 million, liabilities of RM90 million, preferred equity of RM10 million and 20 million ordinary shares outstanding. Ordinary shares have a stated par value of RM1 and trade at RM6. An analyst estimates an investment value of RM7 per share.</p>\n<p>A buyer considers purchasing 100 shares at RM6. The broker charges RM8 to buy and RM8 to sell. Assume the shares are later sold at RM6.30 each, with no dividends or taxes.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Calculate ordinary shareholders' book equity, book value per share and market capitalization.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Distinguish par value, book value, market price and the analyst's investment value. Does the RM7 estimate guarantee profit?</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Calculate the buyer's net gain and holding-period return after the stated commissions. Explain why costs matter especially for frequent trading.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [4] Discuss two additional pieces of information from a quotation or financial statement that would help assess the investment, explaining the purpose of each.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Values [6].</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Common book equity}=RM180m-RM90m-RM10m=RM80m$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Book value per common share}=RM80m/20m=RM4$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Market capitalization}=20m\\times RM6=RM120m$$</div>\n<p>Book equity uses recorded values and subtracts preferred claims for the common share calculation. Market capitalization values the outstanding common shares at the market price; it is not the value of all enterprise claims. <em>Illustrative marking: 2 marks per correct value.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Different questions [5].</strong> Par is a stated accounting/legal value, not a market valuation. RM4 book value per share uses recorded net assets attributable to ordinary shares. RM6 is the current market price per share. RM7 is the analyst's estimated investment value based on assumptions about future benefits and risk. An estimated value above price suggests a potential opportunity only if the estimate is reliable; outcomes can differ and no profit is guaranteed. <em>Illustrative marking: one mark per distinction and one for the limitation.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) After costs [5].</strong> Purchase outlay is $100(6)+8=RM608$. Sale proceeds after commission are $100(6.30)-8=RM622$. Net gain is <strong>RM14</strong> and net holding-period return is $14/608\\approx\\mathbf{2.30\\%}$. Without the two RM8 commissions the price gain would be RM30. Frequent trading repeats costs, so a profitable price movement can be reduced or erased by implementation costs. <em>Illustrative marking: 1 each for purchase, sale, net gain, return and explanation.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Additional information [4].</strong> The bid-ask spread helps assess the cost of trading at currently available prices; wider spreads may make entry and exit more expensive. Earnings, cash flow and debt information help assess financial strength and the assumptions underlying RM7 estimated value. A recent price or yield alone does not establish business quality. <em>Illustrative marking: 2 marks for each relevant item plus why it matters.</em></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><span>Dividends, dates and reinvestment (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Aster reports earnings available to ordinary shareholders of RM12 million and has 6 million ordinary shares outstanding. It pays an annual cash dividend of RM0.80 per share; the current share price is RM16. A shareholder owns 500 shares before the payment. Assume no tax or fees and, for reinvestment only, a share price of RM16 and fractional shares are permitted.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Calculate earnings per share, dividend yield and payout ratio. Explain what each measures and why yield and payout are different.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [4] Calculate the cash dividend received and the number of additional shares if the entire payment is reinvested at the assumed price.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [4] Distinguish declaration, ex-dividend, record and payment dates. Explain why the ex-dividend date matters to a buyer.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [6] Evaluate: \"A company paying the highest dividend yield is necessarily the safest income investment.\" Include the effects of a falling price, future earnings and the investor's needs.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Measures [6].</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$EPS=RM12m/6m=RM2.00\\text{ per share}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Dividend yield}=RM0.80/RM16=5\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Payout ratio}=RM0.80/RM2.00=40\\%$$</div>\n<p>EPS measures earnings attributable to each ordinary share; yield relates annual cash dividend to the current share price; payout relates that dividend to earnings per share. Yield is an income-to-price measure, whereas payout concerns the proportion of earnings distributed. Neither measures the entire investment return. <em>Illustrative marking: 1 mark per numerical result and 1 per corresponding meaning.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Cash or reinvestment [4].</strong> Cash received is $500\\times RM0.80=RM400$. Reinvestment buys $RM400/RM16=25$ shares; the holding becomes <strong>525 shares</strong> under the assumed fractional-share and pricing terms. Reinvestment purchases shares using cash that otherwise could have been withdrawn; it is not an extra dividend. <em>Illustrative marking: cash 1, shares 1, new holding 1, interpretation 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Dates [4].</strong> Declaration: the company announces the dividend. Ex-dividend: the share begins trading without the right to that declared distribution for a new buyer under the applicable market rules. Record: the issuer determines eligible holders on its register. Payment: cash or shares are distributed. Actual settlement rules determine ex-date timing; do not infer a universal number of days from historical slides. <em>Illustrative marking: 1 per accurate date/function.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluation [6].</strong> Reject \"necessarily.\" A dividend yield can rise because the share price has fallen, possibly reflecting deteriorating prospects. A high payout relative to earnings or weak cash generation may be difficult to sustain, although these measures alone cannot prove a cut. Examine earnings, cash flows, debt, dividend history and diversification, then compare the likely income and principal uncertainty with the investor's needs. Equity income is not a contractual or risk-free payment. <em>Illustrative marking: conclusion 1, falling-price mechanism 1, sustainability 2, investor needs and risk 2.</em></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><span>Stock categories and investment strategy (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Consider three hypothetical companies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Oak:</strong> established consumer-staples company with a long dividend record; moderate growth, but dividends are not guaranteed.</li>\n<li><strong>Forge:</strong> industrial-equipment producer whose orders have historically risen and fallen strongly with the economy; it pays a modest dividend.</li>\n<li><strong>Spark:</strong> young technology company with volatile earnings, little dividend income and substantial expansion plans.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Investor A needs reasonably dependable cash for near-term living expenses. Investor B has a long horizon, diversified savings and seeks growth. Investor C wishes to speculate on a possible breakthrough and understands that a large loss is possible.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Describe suitable stock classifications for each company and explain why classifications can overlap.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [6] Propose a reasoned initial match between the three investors and the companies, including an important qualification for each.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [4] Compare buy-and-hold, current-income and aggressive trading strategies in terms of goals, monitoring and costs.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [4] Explain why neither \"blue chip\" nor \"defensive\" means risk-free, and why diversification matters even if an investor chooses a well-known firm.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Analyse → Evaluate → Create.</em></p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Classification [6].</strong> Oak is plausibly an income-oriented stock and may be relatively defensive because essential-goods demand can be less cyclical; its established record may also support a blue-chip description if wider evidence warrants it. Forge is cyclical because its demand moves with industrial conditions. Spark is a technology stock and may be growth-oriented or speculative given uncertain earnings. These describe different dimensions-sector, economic sensitivity, income and track record-so one stock can occupy several categories. <em>Illustrative marking: 1.5 for each classification with reason, 1.5 for overlap.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Investor matching [6].</strong> Oak is the closest of these three to A's cash-income goal, but A should not assume dividends or principal are stable enough for essential near-term spending; other assets and a reserve may be required. B may consider Spark for a limited portion of a diversified growth portfolio, but long horizons do not make one speculative company safe; evidence about its prospects and valuation is required. C's explicit speculation objective aligns most closely with Spark, but C must size the position to a loss they can bear. Forge may be suitable where the investor understands cyclical exposure; no company is automatically the best purchase without price, financial and portfolio information. <em>Illustrative marking: 2 per reasoned match and qualification.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Strategies [4].</strong> Buy-and-hold aims to hold investments over time while still monitoring whether the thesis and needs change. Current-income investing selects assets for cash distributions and their sustainability. Aggressive trading seeks gains through active decisions, requires more monitoring and can incur more spreads, commissions and taxes where applicable. Frequency alone does not ensure better returns. <em>Illustrative marking: 1 each for strategies and 1 for cost/monitoring contrast.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Risk [4].</strong> A blue-chip label describes a track record rather than a guarantee; business conditions can change. Defensive businesses can still lose value, including when interest rates, valuation or company-specific news change. Holding different investments can reduce dependence on one firm's outcome, while leaving market-wide risks. <em>Illustrative marking: 1 each for blue chip and defensive qualifications, 2 for diversification and its limit.</em></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><span>Foreign stock and portfolio decisions (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A Malaysian investor buys a foreign stock for USD100. After one year, the stock sells for USD110 and has paid a USD2 dividend. At purchase, USD1 costs RM4.00; at sale and dividend conversion, USD1 costs RM3.80. Assume the dividend is converted at the end of the year, with no taxes, fees or withholding. All foreign cash flows are converted at those stated rates.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Calculate the local-currency stock return in USD, the ending proceeds including dividend in RM, and the investor's RM return.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [4] Explain the currency movement and why the two returns differ.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [4] Compare purchasing foreign shares directly, holding depositary shares, and buying an international fund. Explain why a depositary share quoted in the investor's trading currency does not necessarily remove exchange-rate exposure.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [6] Design a brief decision process for including foreign equity in a portfolio: discuss diversification, investor constraints, market and currency risk, and costs or accessibility. State one circumstance in which the investor should reduce exposure.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate → Create.</em></p>\n<hr /><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Returns [6].</strong> In USD, total return is $(110-100+2)/100=12\\%$. Initial RM cost is $100\\times4.00=RM400$. Ending value including dividend is $(110+2)\\times3.80=RM425.60$. The RM return is $(425.60-400)/400=\\mathbf{6.4\\%}$. Equivalently, $(1+0.12)(3.80/4.00)-1=6.4\\%$. <em>Illustrative marking: USD return 2, initial cost 1, ending value 1, RM return 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Currency effect [4].</strong> The USD fell from RM4.00 to RM3.80 per USD: it depreciated 5% against the ringgit. Each foreign-currency dollar converts into fewer ringgit, reducing the home-currency return. The combined 6.4% is not simply 12% minus 5% because the stock return and currency return compound. <em>Illustrative marking: direction 1, percentage 1, mechanism 1, compounding 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Access routes [4].</strong> Direct foreign-market purchases give direct access but may involve foreign brokerage, settlement, regulatory, tax and currency considerations. Depositary shares provide a locally traded representation of foreign shares, subject to their particular issuer and market arrangements. An international fund pools investments and may provide diversification, subject to fees and fund-level risks. Quoting a depositary share in a local trading currency does not remove the economic exposure of the underlying foreign business and share price to currencies. <em>Illustrative marking: 1 per route and 1 for currency qualification.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Decision process [6].</strong> First identify the goal, horizon, liquidity requirements and risk capacity. Next examine existing holdings and whether foreign assets add useful exposures; diversification benefits depend on correlations and can vary during stress. Then assess the foreign business and market, currency exposure, political and accounting risks, available information, trading and fund costs, and tax treatment. Choose an appropriately sized route and monitor whether exposure remains suitable. Reduce the allocation if, for example, the investor will need the money soon or a currency loss would exceed their risk capacity. <em>Illustrative marking: objectives 1, diversification 1, risks 2, access and costs 1, justified reduction 1.</em></p>\n<hr /></div></details></div></details></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l6\" id=\"l6s-toc\"><summary><strong>Notes cover</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">6 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"How to demonstrate understanding\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l6s-s1\" title=\"How to demonstrate understanding\">How to demonstrate understanding</button></li><li data-full=\"Mock examination: six 20-mark questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l6s-s2\" title=\"Mock examination: six 20-mark questions\">Mock exam: six 20-mark questions</button></li><li data-full=\"Model answers and illustrative marking guidance\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l6s-s3\" title=\"Model answers and illustrative marking guidance\">Model answers and marking guidance</button></li><li data-full=\"Short-answer revision\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l6s-s4\" title=\"Short-answer revision\">Short-answer revision</button></li><li data-full=\"Formula-selection and decision guide\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l6s-s5\" title=\"Formula-selection and decision guide\">Formula-selection guide</button></li><li data-full=\"Revision priorities and final integrated exercise\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l6s-s6\" title=\"Revision priorities and final integrated exercise\">Priorities and integrated exercise</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l6\" open><summary><div><strong>Complete Chapter 6 subjective notes</strong><small>Every part of the subjective preparation, one dropdown per part.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>6 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><h2>Chapter 6 - Subjective Exam Preparation</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l6s-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">How to demonstrate understanding</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Common-stock questions are rarely answered fully by naming a type of share or calculating a yield. Explain <strong>what the investor owns, how value or income arises, what might go wrong, and why the result matters for this particular investor</strong>.</p>\n<p>A weak answer to \"Why might a retiree prefer a dividend-paying stock?\" is: \"It has a high dividend.\" A stronger answer is: \"A dividend can provide cash to meet current expenses without selling shares. However, the company may reduce its dividend and its share price can fall; the investor should assess dividend sustainability, diversification, and the amount of stable income needed.\" The second response connects the feature to the goal and qualifies the risk.</p>\n<p>Use the command word and marks as a guide: state the principle, explain its mechanism, apply the scenario, show calculations where relevant, and evaluate alternatives or assumptions when requested. The following scenarios, marks, model answers, and priorities are <strong>original practice guidance</strong>, not a verified lecturer's marking scheme. Attempt the questions before looking at the answers.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l6s-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Mock examination: six 20-mark questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Question 1 - Equity ownership and investment performance (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>A company issues ordinary shares to the public. A buyer purchases 200 shares at RM20 each. One year later the shares are worth RM22 each, and the investor has received RM0.60 cash dividend per share. Ignore tax and trading costs.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [4] Explain why ordinary shareholders are called residual owners and compare their claim with that of lenders.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [6] Calculate the total dividend, capital gain, total ringgit return, and holding-period return.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Explain two reasons why the investor's positive one-year result does not guarantee future results. Distinguish company-specific from market-wide risk.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Evaluate the claim: \"Stocks are always the best place for money needed next year because they tend to do well over long periods.\"</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Understand → Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p>\n<h4>Question 2 - Share issuance, rights and corporate actions (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>Meridian has 1,000,000 ordinary shares outstanding. Mira owns 10,000. The company offers existing holders one new share at RM4 for every four shares held. Assume every eligible holder takes up their full entitlement and no other shares are issued.</p>\n<p>Later, after the rights issue, a shareholder holds 2,000 shares trading at RM12 each immediately before a 2-for-1 split. Ignore market movements and costs for the split calculation.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [5] Calculate Mira's percentage ownership before and after fully subscribing. Explain the purpose and a limitation of rights offerings.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [3] Calculate Mira's percentage ownership if all other holders subscribe fully but she buys no new shares.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Calculate the shareholder's post-split number of shares, theoretical price per share and total holding value. Does the split create wealth by itself?</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [4] Explain a spinoff and compare a share repurchase with issuing new shares in terms of outstanding shares and cash flows.</p>\n<p><strong>(e)</strong> [3] Explain why ownership percentage alone may not reveal voting control if common stock has multiple classes.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p>\n<h4>Question 3 - Stock values, quotes and costs (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>Orion has total assets of RM180 million, liabilities of RM90 million, preferred equity of RM10 million and 20 million ordinary shares outstanding. Ordinary shares have a stated par value of RM1 and trade at RM6. An analyst estimates an investment value of RM7 per share.</p>\n<p>A buyer considers purchasing 100 shares at RM6. The broker charges RM8 to buy and RM8 to sell. Assume the shares are later sold at RM6.30 each, with no dividends or taxes.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Calculate ordinary shareholders' book equity, book value per share and market capitalization.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Distinguish par value, book value, market price and the analyst's investment value. Does the RM7 estimate guarantee profit?</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Calculate the buyer's net gain and holding-period return after the stated commissions. Explain why costs matter especially for frequent trading.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [4] Discuss two additional pieces of information from a quotation or financial statement that would help assess the investment, explaining the purpose of each.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p>\n<h4>Question 4 - Dividends, dates and reinvestment (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>Aster reports earnings available to ordinary shareholders of RM12 million and has 6 million ordinary shares outstanding. It pays an annual cash dividend of RM0.80 per share; the current share price is RM16. A shareholder owns 500 shares before the payment. Assume no tax or fees and, for reinvestment only, a share price of RM16 and fractional shares are permitted.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Calculate earnings per share, dividend yield and payout ratio. Explain what each measures and why yield and payout are different.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [4] Calculate the cash dividend received and the number of additional shares if the entire payment is reinvested at the assumed price.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [4] Distinguish declaration, ex-dividend, record and payment dates. Explain why the ex-dividend date matters to a buyer.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [6] Evaluate: \"A company paying the highest dividend yield is necessarily the safest income investment.\" Include the effects of a falling price, future earnings and the investor's needs.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p>\n<h4>Question 5 - Stock categories and investment strategy (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>Consider three hypothetical companies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Oak:</strong> established consumer-staples company with a long dividend record; moderate growth, but dividends are not guaranteed.</li>\n<li><strong>Forge:</strong> industrial-equipment producer whose orders have historically risen and fallen strongly with the economy; it pays a modest dividend.</li>\n<li><strong>Spark:</strong> young technology company with volatile earnings, little dividend income and substantial expansion plans.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Investor A needs reasonably dependable cash for near-term living expenses. Investor B has a long horizon, diversified savings and seeks growth. Investor C wishes to speculate on a possible breakthrough and understands that a large loss is possible.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Describe suitable stock classifications for each company and explain why classifications can overlap.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [6] Propose a reasoned initial match between the three investors and the companies, including an important qualification for each.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [4] Compare buy-and-hold, current-income and aggressive trading strategies in terms of goals, monitoring and costs.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [4] Explain why neither \"blue chip\" nor \"defensive\" means risk-free, and why diversification matters even if an investor chooses a well-known firm.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Analyse → Evaluate → Create.</em></p>\n<h4>Question 6 - Foreign stock and portfolio decisions (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>A Malaysian investor buys a foreign stock for USD100. After one year, the stock sells for USD110 and has paid a USD2 dividend. At purchase, USD1 costs RM4.00; at sale and dividend conversion, USD1 costs RM3.80. Assume the dividend is converted at the end of the year, with no taxes, fees or withholding. All foreign cash flows are converted at those stated rates.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Calculate the local-currency stock return in USD, the ending proceeds including dividend in RM, and the investor's RM return.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [4] Explain the currency movement and why the two returns differ.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [4] Compare purchasing foreign shares directly, holding depositary shares, and buying an international fund. Explain why a depositary share quoted in the investor's trading currency does not necessarily remove exchange-rate exposure.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [6] Design a brief decision process for including foreign equity in a portfolio: discuss diversification, investor constraints, market and currency risk, and costs or accessibility. State one circumstance in which the investor should reduce exposure.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate → Create.</em></p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l6s-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Model answers and illustrative marking guidance</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Model answer 1 - Ownership and returns</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Residual claim [4].</strong> Ordinary shareholders own the equity remaining after the firm's obligations and senior claims. Lenders have contractual claims to interest and repayment; shareholders do not have a comparable guarantee of dividend or principal. If the company is liquidated, shareholders receive value only after prior claims are met. Shareholders may benefit from growth in the residual value, but could lose their investment. <em>Illustrative marking: 2 marks for the claim and 2 for an accurate lender comparison.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Returns [6].</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Initial investment = $200\\times RM20=RM4,000$.</li>\n<li>Dividend = $200\\times RM0.60=RM120$.</li>\n<li>Capital gain = $200\\times(RM22-RM20)=RM400$.</li>\n<li>Total return = $RM120+RM400=RM520$.</li>\n<li>Holding-period return = $RM520/RM4,000=13\\%$.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The 13% includes 10% price appreciation plus 3% dividend income, based on the initial price. <em>Illustrative marking: dividend 1, capital gain 1, total 1, formula and result 2, interpretation 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Uncertainty [5].</strong> The company might lose a major customer or its management might make a poor decision: these are company-specific risks. An economy-wide recession or market repricing can affect many companies simultaneously: this is market-wide risk. Diversifying may reduce company-specific exposure but cannot remove market-wide risk. Last year's price change and dividend are historical observations, not promises about next year. <em>Illustrative marking: 2 for each risk with mechanism, 1 for the historical-return limitation.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Decision [5].</strong> Reject the absolute claim. Long-run historical performance does not prevent a substantial decline over a particular year. If the money must be available next year, liquidity and the ability to tolerate loss matter more than simply maximizing expected stock return. A diversified stock allocation might still have a role for money with a longer horizon, depending on circumstances; a near-term spending reserve requires a separate assessment of lower-risk alternatives and purchasing-power risk. <em>Illustrative marking: conclusion 1, horizon/liquidity 2, risk and qualified alternative 2.</em></p>\n<h4>Model answer 2 - Corporate actions</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Rights [5].</strong> Initially Mira owns $10,000/1,000,000=1\\%$. Her entitlement is $10,000/4=2,500$ shares, costing $2,500\\times RM4=RM10,000$. All holders together subscribe to $1,000,000/4=250,000$ shares. Afterward she owns $12,500$ out of $1,250,000$, still <strong>1%</strong>. A rights offering lets existing owners participate proportionally in new financing and can limit ownership dilution if they subscribe; participation requires cash, and the market value of the right or eventual value of the new shares is not guaranteed. <em>Illustrative marking: entitlement 1, totals 1, both ownership percentages 1, purpose 1, limitation 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) No subscription [3].</strong> Other holders own 990,000 shares and subscribe to $990,000/4=247,500$ new shares. Total outstanding become $1,247,500$; Mira retains 10,000 and owns $10,000/1,247,500\\approx\\mathbf{0.8016\\%}$. This decrease is ownership dilution. The calculation assumes Mira's unsubscribed entitlement is not reallocated; a real offering's terms may differ. <em>Illustrative marking: new share count 1, denominator 1, percentage and interpretation 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Split [5].</strong> The 2,000 shares become 4,000. The theoretical price halves to RM6, so the value remains $4,000\\times RM6=RM24,000$, the same as $2,000\\times RM12$. A split changes units, not the company's assets or the investor's proportional ownership by itself. Actual trading prices can subsequently change. <em>Illustrative marking: shares 1, price 1, value 1, mechanism 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Spinoff and buyback [4].</strong> A spinoff separates a subsidiary into an independent company, distributing its shares to existing owners under the transaction terms. A company issuing new shares generally receives capital and increases outstanding shares; repurchasing shares generally pays out company cash and reduces shares outstanding while held in treasury or retired, depending on rules and treatment. Effects on per-share value depend on price and financing, not solely on share count. <em>Illustrative marking: spinoff 2, issuance versus buyback 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(e) Voting [3].</strong> Classes can have different votes per share or other rights. A small economic ownership percentage can therefore command a disproportionately large voting percentage. Read the class terms before inferring control. <em>Illustrative marking: differentiated rights 2, implication 1.</em></p>\n<h4>Model answer 3 - Values and transaction costs</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Values [6].</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Common book equity}=RM180m-RM90m-RM10m=RM80m$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Book value per common share}=RM80m/20m=RM4$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Market capitalization}=20m\\times RM6=RM120m$$</div>\n<p>Book equity uses recorded values and subtracts preferred claims for the common share calculation. Market capitalization values the outstanding common shares at the market price; it is not the value of all enterprise claims. <em>Illustrative marking: 2 marks per correct value.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Different questions [5].</strong> Par is a stated accounting/legal value, not a market valuation. RM4 book value per share uses recorded net assets attributable to ordinary shares. RM6 is the current market price per share. RM7 is the analyst's estimated investment value based on assumptions about future benefits and risk. An estimated value above price suggests a potential opportunity only if the estimate is reliable; outcomes can differ and no profit is guaranteed. <em>Illustrative marking: one mark per distinction and one for the limitation.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) After costs [5].</strong> Purchase outlay is $100(6)+8=RM608$. Sale proceeds after commission are $100(6.30)-8=RM622$. Net gain is <strong>RM14</strong> and net holding-period return is $14/608\\approx\\mathbf{2.30\\%}$. Without the two RM8 commissions the price gain would be RM30. Frequent trading repeats costs, so a profitable price movement can be reduced or erased by implementation costs. <em>Illustrative marking: 1 each for purchase, sale, net gain, return and explanation.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Additional information [4].</strong> The bid-ask spread helps assess the cost of trading at currently available prices; wider spreads may make entry and exit more expensive. Earnings, cash flow and debt information help assess financial strength and the assumptions underlying RM7 estimated value. A recent price or yield alone does not establish business quality. <em>Illustrative marking: 2 marks for each relevant item plus why it matters.</em></p>\n<h4>Model answer 4 - Dividend analysis</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Measures [6].</strong></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$EPS=RM12m/6m=RM2.00\\text{ per share}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Dividend yield}=RM0.80/RM16=5\\%$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Payout ratio}=RM0.80/RM2.00=40\\%$$</div>\n<p>EPS measures earnings attributable to each ordinary share; yield relates annual cash dividend to the current share price; payout relates that dividend to earnings per share. Yield is an income-to-price measure, whereas payout concerns the proportion of earnings distributed. Neither measures the entire investment return. <em>Illustrative marking: 1 mark per numerical result and 1 per corresponding meaning.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Cash or reinvestment [4].</strong> Cash received is $500\\times RM0.80=RM400$. Reinvestment buys $RM400/RM16=25$ shares; the holding becomes <strong>525 shares</strong> under the assumed fractional-share and pricing terms. Reinvestment purchases shares using cash that otherwise could have been withdrawn; it is not an extra dividend. <em>Illustrative marking: cash 1, shares 1, new holding 1, interpretation 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Dates [4].</strong> Declaration: the company announces the dividend. Ex-dividend: the share begins trading without the right to that declared distribution for a new buyer under the applicable market rules. Record: the issuer determines eligible holders on its register. Payment: cash or shares are distributed. Actual settlement rules determine ex-date timing; do not infer a universal number of days from historical slides. <em>Illustrative marking: 1 per accurate date/function.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluation [6].</strong> Reject \"necessarily.\" A dividend yield can rise because the share price has fallen, possibly reflecting deteriorating prospects. A high payout relative to earnings or weak cash generation may be difficult to sustain, although these measures alone cannot prove a cut. Examine earnings, cash flows, debt, dividend history and diversification, then compare the likely income and principal uncertainty with the investor's needs. Equity income is not a contractual or risk-free payment. <em>Illustrative marking: conclusion 1, falling-price mechanism 1, sustainability 2, investor needs and risk 2.</em></p>\n<h4>Model answer 5 - Categories and strategies</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Classification [6].</strong> Oak is plausibly an income-oriented stock and may be relatively defensive because essential-goods demand can be less cyclical; its established record may also support a blue-chip description if wider evidence warrants it. Forge is cyclical because its demand moves with industrial conditions. Spark is a technology stock and may be growth-oriented or speculative given uncertain earnings. These describe different dimensions-sector, economic sensitivity, income and track record-so one stock can occupy several categories. <em>Illustrative marking: 1.5 for each classification with reason, 1.5 for overlap.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Investor matching [6].</strong> Oak is the closest of these three to A's cash-income goal, but A should not assume dividends or principal are stable enough for essential near-term spending; other assets and a reserve may be required. B may consider Spark for a limited portion of a diversified growth portfolio, but long horizons do not make one speculative company safe; evidence about its prospects and valuation is required. C's explicit speculation objective aligns most closely with Spark, but C must size the position to a loss they can bear. Forge may be suitable where the investor understands cyclical exposure; no company is automatically the best purchase without price, financial and portfolio information. <em>Illustrative marking: 2 per reasoned match and qualification.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Strategies [4].</strong> Buy-and-hold aims to hold investments over time while still monitoring whether the thesis and needs change. Current-income investing selects assets for cash distributions and their sustainability. Aggressive trading seeks gains through active decisions, requires more monitoring and can incur more spreads, commissions and taxes where applicable. Frequency alone does not ensure better returns. <em>Illustrative marking: 1 each for strategies and 1 for cost/monitoring contrast.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Risk [4].</strong> A blue-chip label describes a track record rather than a guarantee; business conditions can change. Defensive businesses can still lose value, including when interest rates, valuation or company-specific news change. Holding different investments can reduce dependence on one firm's outcome, while leaving market-wide risks. <em>Illustrative marking: 1 each for blue chip and defensive qualifications, 2 for diversification and its limit.</em></p>\n<h4>Model answer 6 - Foreign returns and portfolio choice</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Returns [6].</strong> In USD, total return is $(110-100+2)/100=12\\%$. Initial RM cost is $100\\times4.00=RM400$. Ending value including dividend is $(110+2)\\times3.80=RM425.60$. The RM return is $(425.60-400)/400=\\mathbf{6.4\\%}$. Equivalently, $(1+0.12)(3.80/4.00)-1=6.4\\%$. <em>Illustrative marking: USD return 2, initial cost 1, ending value 1, RM return 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Currency effect [4].</strong> The USD fell from RM4.00 to RM3.80 per USD: it depreciated 5% against the ringgit. Each foreign-currency dollar converts into fewer ringgit, reducing the home-currency return. The combined 6.4% is not simply 12% minus 5% because the stock return and currency return compound. <em>Illustrative marking: direction 1, percentage 1, mechanism 1, compounding 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Access routes [4].</strong> Direct foreign-market purchases give direct access but may involve foreign brokerage, settlement, regulatory, tax and currency considerations. Depositary shares provide a locally traded representation of foreign shares, subject to their particular issuer and market arrangements. An international fund pools investments and may provide diversification, subject to fees and fund-level risks. Quoting a depositary share in a local trading currency does not remove the economic exposure of the underlying foreign business and share price to currencies. <em>Illustrative marking: 1 per route and 1 for currency qualification.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Decision process [6].</strong> First identify the goal, horizon, liquidity requirements and risk capacity. Next examine existing holdings and whether foreign assets add useful exposures; diversification benefits depend on correlations and can vary during stress. Then assess the foreign business and market, currency exposure, political and accounting risks, available information, trading and fund costs, and tax treatment. Choose an appropriately sized route and monitor whether exposure remains suitable. Reduce the allocation if, for example, the investor will need the money soon or a currency loss would exceed their risk capacity. <em>Illustrative marking: objectives 1, diversification 1, risks 2, access and costs 1, justified reduction 1.</em></p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l6s-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Short-answer revision</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><ol>\n<li>Why is a common shareholder's claim described as residual?</li>\n<li>How can a positive dividend accompany a negative total holding-period return?</li>\n<li>What changes and what does not automatically change in a 2-for-1 stock split?</li>\n<li>How could a rights offering reduce dilution for a shareholder who subscribes?</li>\n<li>Why can market value per share differ from book value per share?</li>\n<li>How can dividend yield rise while a company's prospects deteriorate?</li>\n<li>Why is a dividend reinvestment plan different from a stock dividend?</li>\n<li>Why may a growth stock also be described as a technology stock?</li>\n<li>How can a foreign stock gain in its own currency yet produce a loss in ringgit?</li>\n<li>Why do frequent trades need to earn enough to overcome transaction costs?</li>\n</ol></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l6s-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Formula-selection and decision guide</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l6_four_values\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Par, book, market and investment value answer four different questions; none of them has to equal another.</figcaption></figure></div><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>If asked for ...</th>\n<th>Start with ...</th>\n<th>Common mistake</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Total return</td>\n<td>$(P_1-P_0+D)/P_0$</td>\n<td>Ignoring dividends or treating price gain as total return.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Market capitalization</td>\n<td>Outstanding common shares x market price</td>\n<td>Confusing share price with the whole company's market capitalization.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Common book value per share</td>\n<td>$(\\text{assets}-\\text{liabilities}-\\text{preferred equity})/\\text{common shares}$</td>\n<td>Treating accounting value as a guaranteed sale price.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dividend yield</td>\n<td>Annual dividend per share / current price per share</td>\n<td>Confusing it with payout or guaranteed future return.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dividend payout</td>\n<td>Annual dividend per share / EPS</td>\n<td>Dividing by market price instead of earnings.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Home-currency return</td>\n<td>Convert ending value and income to home currency, then compare with initial home-currency cost</td>\n<td>Ignoring the exchange rate or omitting the interaction of returns.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Use the specific exercise's timing and cost assumptions; reinvestment, taxes and changing exchange rates can require additional steps.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l6s-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Revision priorities and final integrated exercise</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Practise first the distinctions between shareholder and creditor claims, dividend and price returns, market and book value, yield and payout, and stock characteristics versus investor suitability. Then apply them together rather than memorizing labels in isolation.</p>\n<p><strong>Integrated exercise:</strong> An investor who needs income in two years proposes placing all savings in one high-yield foreign technology stock because \"its dividend is 8%, its price has fallen, and it is quoted in ringgit.\" Evaluate the proposal and design a more appropriate decision process.</p>\n<p>A strong answer would ask whether essential near-term spending can tolerate a decline or dividend cut; investigate whether the falling price signals deteriorating prospects and whether the payout is supported by earnings and cash; assess the company's valuation and concentration risk; and explain that a ringgit quote can still embed foreign-currency exposure. Compare diversified alternatives and a suitable liquidity reserve against the investor's constraints. The 8% yield is neither total return nor a guarantee.</p>\n<p><em>Source note:</em> Based on the concepts in <code>pdf/</code> (Pearson, 2014). All questions and calculations above are original practice material. Historical returns, tax figures and examples in the slides are period-specific; no current tax rule or exam marking scheme is asserted here.</p></div></details></div></details>");
